  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather is expected the majority of the time \nthrough early next week. However, a few rain showers may \naccompany the passage of a cold front this Monday.\n\nAloft, a ridge continues to build from the western Great Lakes\nand vicinity through tonight and then settles over the Northeast\nUnited States and vicinity this Sunday through Sunday night. At\nthe surface, the ridge continues to affect our region as the\nembedded high pressure center wobbles generally S'ward from\nsouthern QC to the Mid-Atlantic states. Dry weather persists in\nour CWA as stabilizing subsidence accompanies the ridge. Sky\nwill be clear during most of the time period, but passing\ncloudiness, primarily in the mid/upper-levels, is expected at\ntimes as moist isentropic ascent precedes the axes of shortwave\ntroughs embedded in the primarily W'erly to NW'erly flow aloft,\nespecially before daybreak this morning and again Sunday\nafternoon through Sunday night. Lows should reach mainly the \nmid 20's to mid 30's around daybreak this morning as clearing \nsky, weak or calm surface winds, and low humidity promote \nefficient radiational cooling. Highs should reach the mid 50's \nto lower 60's late this afternoon as weak WAA begins to develop\nalong the western flank of the low-level ridge. Overnight lows \nmainly in the mid 20's to upper 30's are forecast around \ndaybreak Sunday due to another round of efficient radiational \ncooling. Warmer highs, mainly in the upper 50's to upper 60's, \nare expected on Sunday and should be followed by overnight lows \nmainly in the mid 30's to upper 40's around daybreak Monday as \nthe WAA regime strengthens somewhat along the western flank of \nthe low-level ridge.\n\nOn Monday, cyclonic W'erly to WNW'erly flow aloft and embedded \nshortwave troughs are expected to impact our region. Variable\namounts of mainly mid/upper-level cloudiness are expected amidst\nmoist isentropic ascent ahead of the shortwave trough axes. At \nthe surface, the aforementioned ridge will exit generally \nSE'ward and be followed by the SE'ward passage of a weak cold \nfront during the late morning through early afternoon. A rather \ndry low-level atmospheric column should permit a dry cold front \npassage across most of our CWA. However, forcing for ascent\nalong the front may coincide with sufficient amounts of low-level\nmoisture to permit the development of isolated and light rain \nshowers across far-northern portions of Jefferson and Lewis \nCounties, including the Thousand Islands. Behind the front, \nanother high pressure ridge builds from central Canada and \nvicinity. Late afternoon highs should reach mainly the mid 50's\nto mid 60's.\n\nDuring Monday night through Tuesday, the surface ridge will\ncontinue to affect our region as the embedded high pressure\ncenter moves from northern ON toward southwestern QC, while the\nridge aloft builds from the Upper Midwest and eventually the\nwestern and central Great Lakes. Dry weather is expected as\nstabilizing subsidence accompanies the ridge. Low-level CAA\nbehind Monday's cold front passage will contribute to lows\nreaching mainly the lower 20's to mid 30's around daybreak\nTuesday and highs reaching the 40's to 50's late Tuesday\nafternoon.\n\nNote: Clear sky or sufficient cloud breaks and weak or calm\nsurface winds should contribute to localized steam fog in the \nBlack River Valley, Western Southern Tier river valleys, and the\nlake valleys of the Finger Lakes Region during the predawn \nhours through about 10 AM EDT today through Tuesday, respectively.",
